   $q$The Greek prefix epi- means "upon" or "over," and derm means "skin." Why is "epidermis" a fitting name for the layer it describes?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It is the layer of skin that lies under the dermis","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"It is the layer of skin that holds the color cells","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-nearby-details"},{"text":"It is the layer of skin that lies over the dermis","is_correct":true},{"text":"It is the layer of skin that is thickest and strongest","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It is the layer of skin that lies over the dermis$q$, $q$Epi- means "upon," so the epidermis is the skin layer on top, matching "the outer layer" in the passage, with the dermis "beneath it." Saying it holds the color cells describes the dermis instead.$q$, 'draft', '71faa34b-bb27-563d-a6f9-253341a2d3a5')
