  ('0c123cc7-248f-5fc3-aa07-f49f06be7463', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-word-parts'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The prefix inter- means "between," and the Latin root sect means "cut." Why is "intersection" a fitting word for where the transit center will go?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It is the spot where an old road was cut off and closed","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It is the spot where the town is cut into two halves","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-broad-context"},{"text":"It is the spot where the two roads cut across each other","is_correct":true},{"text":"It is the spot between the downtown and the suburbs","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-root-clues"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It is the spot where the two roads cut across each other$q$, $q$Inter- plus sect means "cut between," and an intersection is where roads cross, here the junction of Route 9 and Mill Road. Being between downtown and the suburbs uses only inter- and drops the idea of roads crossing.$q$, 'draft', '8322960e-ac2d-515a-aa45-ae32e3bdf95f')
