   $q$The Latin root nav means "ship." What did sailors need the knots for when they had to "navigate safely"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To pull old ropes up from the bottom of a harbor","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"context-only-guess"},{"text":"To guide and steer their ships from place to place","is_correct":true},{"text":"To repair torn sails after a storm had passed","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"To build new ships in a long building on the shore","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-nearby-details"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To guide and steer their ships from place to place$q$, $q$Nav means "ship," and the sentence explains navigating as "steering their ships through storms and tying them securely in strange ports." The long building, the ropewalk, is where rope was made, not where ships were guided.$q$, 'draft', 'd9f9c526-c701-5695-880f-42fe4452c2d2')
